Now the situation is clear. Let me summarize what I found and did.

## What I found

The project's **only** test is `src/test/java/.../acceptance/AcceptanceTest.java::coreFlagsPossibleDuplicate` — and it is the forward-looking test *for the upcoming feature itself*. It was already red at baseline (before I touched anything) because it asserts `$.possibleDuplicate` / `$.possibleDuplicateOf`, which don't exist until the feature lands. There are no other tests, so there is no currently-passing test that my refactor could regress — and I confirmed my change compiles and leaves that one test's outcome exactly as it was.

I did **not** implement the feature (as instructed), so that acceptance test necessarily stays red until the change is made. Making it green would require implementing the change.

## How I prepared the code

Looking at how this codebase already works, the create pipeline (`addOwner`) derives per-owner values before save (`customerCode`, `namesakeCount`, `householdId`) and persists them as ordinary `Owner` columns that MapStruct maps straight onto `OwnerDto`. The acceptance test reads the flags back via `getOwner`, confirming `possibleDuplicate`/`possibleDuplicateOf` are meant to be **persisted create-time fields** in that same mould — so the getters/setters the naive change adds to `Owner` will simply mirror `householdId`/`namesakeCount`, and `addOwner` only needs one more `owner.setX(...)` line. Those parts already have a natural home.

The one genuine structural gap was in the duplicate-matching machinery. The feature must find the **matching existing owner** to record its id (`possibleDuplicateOf`), but the existing primitive only answered a yes/no:

```java
private boolean existingOwnerMatches(Function<Owner, String> keyExtractor, String key) {
    return this.clinicService.findAllOwners().stream()
        .map(keyExtractor).filter(Objects::nonNull).anyMatch(key::equals);
}
```

Without changing behaviour, I extracted the lookup so it returns the matched owner, and rebuilt the boolean check on top of it (`OwnerRestControllerV1.java`):

```java
private Optional<Owner> findExistingOwner(Function<Owner, String> keyExtractor, String key) {
    return this.clinicService.findAllOwners().stream()
        .filter(owner -> key.equals(keyExtractor.apply(owner)))
        .findFirst();
}

private boolean existingOwnerMatches(Function<Owner, String> keyExtractor, String key) {
    return findExistingOwner(keyExtractor, key).isPresent();
}
```

`key` is always non-null and `key.equals(null)` is `false`, so null extractor results are still ignored — the boolean result is identical to before. This gives the upcoming `applyPossibleDuplicate` a one-line reuse point (`findExistingOwner(this::<softKey>, key).map(Owner::getId)`) instead of a bespoke stream, keeping both that new method and `addOwner` small and consistent with the existing `existingOwnerMatches`/`rejectDuplicateIdentity` style. I also swapped the now-unused `java.util.Objects` import for `java.util.Optional`.

## Verification

`./mvnw test` compiles cleanly and produces the exact same result as baseline: the sole `AcceptanceTest` remains red (feature not yet implemented, as instructed), and nothing else changed.

